course: dictionary
lesson: 14086
title: Medication: medicine with a purpose
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Medication is a more formal word for medicine. It means pills, liquids, creams or injections that a doctor gives you to treat an illness or stop pain. «The nurse gave Sam his medication after lunch.» Here the nurse is the grown-up who gave it, and the word tells you it was medicine for a health problem.
explain: You can also use medication for the act of taking medicine regularly. «Grandad is on medication for his heart» means he takes medicine every day, as the doctor has told him to.
explain: Medication is always for a body that needs help. Context clues such as «doctor», «nurse» and «illness» tell you it is about health. Try swapping in medicine: if the sentence still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
fact: Medication means medicine: pills, liquids, creams or injections.
fact: Medication is given to treat an illness or to stop pain.
fact: «Medication» is a slightly more formal word than «medicine».
fact: «Keep all medication out of reach of small children.» This is an important safety rule.
fact: Only take medication given to you by a parent, doctor or school nurse.
exercise: «The nurse gave Sam his medication after lunch.» What is medication here? | A type of exercise | A game played at break time | A snack | *Medicine that a doctor or nurse gives to treat an illness
exercise: «Keep all medication out of reach of small children.» Why must medication be kept away from small children? | Because it smells nice | *Because it can harm them if they take it by mistake | Because it is heavy to carry | Because it is a toy
exercise: «Grandad is on medication for his heart.» Find and copy one word that means medicine. | Grandad | is | *medication | heart
test: Which of these is the best reason to take medication? | Because a friend has some and says it is fun | *Because a doctor or nurse has told you to take it | Because it tastes sweet | Because you saw it in a shop
test: Mia says, «Medication is just a fancy word for sweets.» Is she right? Why? | Yes, because medication tastes nice | Yes, because doctors give out sweets | *No, because medication is medicine for an illness or pain, not sweets | No, because medication is always a drink
exam: Ravi says, «I will take the pills my friend gave me for my headache.» What should he do and why? | Take them quickly before his headache gets worse | Keep them in his pocket until he forgets about them | Share them with his brother | *Tell a grown-up, because only medication given by a parent, doctor or nurse is safe to take
